Why: a host restore policy (focus_steal::set_restore_policy) asked before every restore the driver makes, so an embedding app can leave the user's own switches and system prompts in front.

The driver puts the user's app back in front from three places: the
activation observer (focus_steal, used by actions and by launch_app's
watchdog), the self-activation revert and the window-order restore
(key_focus_guard, also used by the browser launch watch). Each now builds a
RestoreRequest (kind, the app that came forward, the app to restore, the
named target or None for a wildcard entry, when the guard began, origin) and
skips the restore when the installed policy returns false. Without a policy
nothing changes. FocusStealPreventer::restores_for_activation,
key_focus_guard::would_revert_self_activation and order_restore_allowed
expose the decisions without restoring, for tests. Upstreamable as is.

